// bed's surface and the glass where they meet inside it, so its own top (glass bottom at its\r
// centre + thickness) says little about where the surface runs: the glass at its centre may\r
// lie well above the bed. Taken at face value it bends the surface up the glass (a fin at\r
// every rim column, as the rim crosses the columns: a comb). A rim column instead takes the\r
// bed's surface carried on from the sand further in, where that is lower. Bare columns are\r
// marked for the extension pass. The sand lies on the floor: the glass, or the reef rock where it\r
// rises above it (rock_bed.wgsl). Where the rock is bare its own top is the surface.\r
