`,H$=`// Particle shifting: keeps the particles evenly spread (after Lind et al. 2012).\r
//\r
// FLIP particles only follow the grid's flow, and the grid (2x2x2 particles per cell) cannot\r
// see how they are arranged inside a cell: once a splash has scrambled them they stay\r
// scrambled, and the smoothed density they make, which is the water surface, keeps their\r
// irregularity. The water then never settles mirror-flat. Here each particle moves a little\r
// down the gradient of the smoothed density, from crowded to sparse. Near the free surface\r
// only the part along the surface is kept, so the water neither spreads into the air nor\r
// swells; there it moves water from bumps into dips, as surface tension does on scales the\r
// grid cannot resolve.\r
